---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyTimeCard from '@/components/tools/datetime/CalcMoneyTimeCard.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hours-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyTimeCard client:load variant="hours" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Subtracting times is harder than it looks</h2>
    <p>
      Clock time is base sixty but looks decimal, so mental subtraction often goes wrong. From 09:15 to
      17:40 is not 17.40 − 9.15 = 8.25. It is eight hours and twenty-five minutes, which is 8.42 as a
      decimal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reliable manual method: convert both times to minutes since midnight, subtract, then convert
      back. 17:40 is 1,060 minutes, 09:15 is 555, and the difference is 505 minutes, which is 8 hours
      25. This hours calculator does the same for each row.
    </p>

    <h2>Add as many periods as you need</h2>
    <p>
      Time-keeping is rarely one pair of times. A day split around a client meeting, a project worked on
      three times in a week, or a freelance invoice for six sessions each gets its own labelled row, and
      the totals add up across all rows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Give each row a clear label. If someone questions the total three weeks later, the labels show
      what each period was.
    </p>

    <h2>Type times however you like</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[380px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>You type</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Understood as</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>9</td><td style="text-align:right">09:00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>930</td><td style="text-align:right">09:30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9:30</td><td style="text-align:right">09:30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9 30</td><td style="text-align:right">09:30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9:30 pm</td><td style="text-align:right">21:30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5p</td><td style="text-align:right">17:00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1745</td><td style="text-align:right">17:45</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12am</td><td style="text-align:right">00:00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Breaks and overnight periods</h2>
    <p>
      Each row has an unpaid break in minutes, which the tool deducts from that row before the total. If
      the end time is at or before the start time, the period crosses midnight, so 23:00 to 02:30 is
      three and a half hours and not a negative number.
    </p>

    <h2>h:mm and decimal, side by side</h2>
    <p>
      <code>h:mm</code> is how a clock and a rota show time. Decimal hours (hours plus minutes divided by
      sixty) are what payroll systems, invoicing software and spreadsheets need, because you cannot
      multiply 8:25 by an hourly rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each row and the total show both formats, plus the figure in minutes for billing in six-minute or
      fifteen-minute units.
    </p>

    <h2>Common uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        For freelance invoicing, add a row per session and set your rate. The gross amount shows with
        the hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        To check a payslip, rebuild the week from your own notes and compare the decimal total with the
        hours you were paid for.
      </li>
      <li>
        For project time, total the hours on one piece of work across several days.
      </li>
      <li>
        For study and practice logs, find how many hours a task took.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a full Monday-to-Sunday grid with daily and weekly overtime rules, the time card calculator
      uses the same engine in a week layout.
    </p>

    <h2>Rounding, if your client or employer uses it</h2>
    <p>
      Rounding to the nearest quarter or tenth of an hour applies per entry, before the total, and
      always to the nearest increment in both directions. Legal billing usually uses tenths (six
      minutes). US payroll usually uses quarters. On "exact", nothing is rounded.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing leaves the tab</h2>
    <p>
      Your working hours are personal data: they show where you were and when. This page runs in your
      browser, saves nothing unless you switch saving on, and makes the CSV, Excel and PDF exports
      locally.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
